## Why it matters

Emirates is one of the largest international recruiters of cabin crew, and its hiring runs in waves city by city rather than through one global deadline. That matters for applicants from Nepal and South Asia: there is rarely a single "last date" to miss. Instead, the opportunity moves — an open day in one city this week, an invite-only event in another city next week, and online applications open in between. The airline has been expanding routes, including new premium-economy services and continued A350 rollouts, and cabin crew hiring tends to track that route expansion. For a Nepali applicant, the practical point is simple: you do not wait for a Kathmandu event. You apply online, and you can attend an open day in another country.

## Who can apply

Emirates publishes a clear baseline, and recruitment guides repeat it consistently. Applicants must be at least 21 years old at the time of joining, at least 160 cm tall, and able to reach 212 cm on tiptoes — the reach test exists because crew must operate overhead emergency equipment. A high-school diploma (Grade 12 or equivalent) is the minimum education, and fluent spoken and written English is required; additional languages are an advantage. Applicants need at least one year of hospitality or customer-service experience, must have no visible tattoos while in Emirates cabin crew uniform, and must be willing to relocate to Dubai for approximately eight weeks of paid training. Meeting every box does not guarantee selection — thousands apply every month and only a small share receive the invitation to join — but missing even one usually means automatic rejection at the first screening, so check twice before travelling.

## How to apply

There are two routes in, and understanding the difference saves a wasted trip. First, the open day: a walk-in recruitment day where you arrive on time with your CV and photos, meet a recruiter, and the process begins the same day. No prior registration is needed in most locations. Second, the invite-only event: you apply online first through the Emirates Group Careers website and wait for a formal invitation. The South Africa events follow this second model — applicants must submit an online application and be selected before attending. If there is no event near you, Emirates says to apply online anyway, and it will let you know when it is visiting nearby. You can also attend an open day in another country.

Prepare before you go. Emirates asks for a recent CV in English and a recent photo; the online form starts with the CV (PDF or Word, up to 2 MB), then general information, then a few job questions. Put hospitality or customer-service experience near the top of the CV with dates, because it is a stated requirement. Candidates who apply online report being sent a recorded video interview, often on HireVue, before being invited to a recruitment day — prepare for it like a final interview: short, clear answers, looking at the camera. At an open day, expect a welcome and introduction, group exercises and an online test at the venue, and a one-on-one final interview. Emirates says it does not offer online assessment days at the moment: if someone offers you one, check it on the official page first.

## What the job pays and provides

Emirates does not publish a single starting salary figure in its advertisements, and this article does not invent one. The airline describes pay as three components: a fixed basic salary, hourly pay for operated flights, and an overseas meal allowance, with salaries tax-free. Listed benefits include concessional travel, accommodation, transport, leave and leave travel, and medical and insurance provisions. Successful candidates relocate to Dubai for employment and training.

## What to do next

First, confirm the event you plan to attend on emiratesgroupcareers.com/cabin-crew — the portal, not a social-media post, is the source of truth for dates and venues. Second, apply online even if you plan to walk into an open day, because some events are invite-only and online applicants are considered for invitations. Third, never pay anyone for an Emirates application, assessment or training place: Emirates never charges candidates, and anyone asking for money is running a scam. If an agent promises a guaranteed seat for a fee, walk away and report the account. For readers in Nepal, the honest route is the official portal and, if you can travel, an open day in a nearby country — not a paid shortcut that does not exist.$$,
