## Why it matters

Category-based selection is how Canada now targets shortages directly. Instead of inviting only the highest CRS scores overall, IRCC runs rounds for specific occupation groups — and healthcare and social services is one of the few categories with repeated rounds in 2026. The September round alone invited 3,500 people at CRS 475, a cut-off below the Canadian Experience Class range reported in the same period (around 515–520). For a nurse, pharmacist or social worker whose overall CRS sits below the general draws, the category round is the realistic route in: the ranking happens inside the category, not against every candidate in the pool.

The category also sits inside a much larger 2026 picture. IRCC has issued well over 130,000 invitations in 2026 across all categories, with French-language draws the largest single block, followed by the Canadian Experience Class and provincial nominees. Healthcare's 11,500 is a smaller share, but it is concentrated: three rounds, all in one occupation family, all inviting thousands at a time. That concentration is what makes it worth watching for health workers specifically.

## Who is eligible

The category covers the health and social-service occupations on IRCC's eligible list. The list includes registered nurses and registered psychiatric nurses, nurse practitioners, dentists, dental hygienists and therapists, pharmacists, physiotherapists, occupational therapists, psychologists, social workers, medical laboratory technologists, medical radiation technologists, respiratory therapists, paramedical occupations, and several more — more than thirty occupations in total. Two rules decide eligibility, and both are strict. First, you need an Express Entry profile under one of the federal programmes (Federal Skilled Worker, Canadian Experience Class, or Federal Skilled Trades, as applicable). Second, your qualifying experience must be in a single eligible occupation — not a mix of related roles. Category rounds do not replace the CRS: IRCC still ranks eligible candidates by CRS and invites from the top down until the round's invitation target is met.

## What the September round shows

The September round also clarifies where healthcare sits relative to other categories. French-language draws have been the largest block of 2026, with ten rounds and 50,500 invitations and cut-offs as low as 382 — the lowest of any 2026 draw of any kind. That matters for health workers who also speak French: strong French scores add 25 to 50 bonus CRS points on top of category eligibility, which can lift an ordinary profile into invitation range even outside healthcare rounds. For a health worker without French, the healthcare category itself remains the lever — 3,500 invitations at CRS 475 in a single round is a large, targeted intake that general draws do not offer.

## How to apply

There is no separate application form for the category. The route is: create an Express Entry profile on the official IRCC website, making sure your occupation, work history and language results are entered accurately; if your occupation is on the healthcare and social services list and you meet the programme criteria, you are automatically considered for category rounds. When a round invites you, you receive an Invitation to Apply and then submit the full permanent-residence application with documents. Because rounds are announced without a fixed calendar, the practical step is keeping the profile complete and up to date — an outdated profile, an expired language test, or a miscoded occupation can cost a round. Processing for the programme is reported at about six months or less once the full application is submitted.

## What to do next

First, check your occupation against IRCC's current eligible list on the official site — lists are revised, and this article's examples are not a substitute for the official list. Second, if you qualify, build or update the Express Entry profile now rather than waiting for a round announcement; rounds reward candidates who are already in the pool. Third, treat any agent promising a guaranteed invitation or a specific draw date as a red flag: IRCC announces rounds itself, and no agent controls selection. For Nepali health workers, the realistic path is the federal category round or a provincial stream that targets health occupations — both run through official channels, and both are free to enter at the profile stage.$$,
