    "bodyEn" = $$AI-linked layoffs are no longer a Silicon Valley story — and the count keeps growing. A Reuters factbox tracking AI-linked job cuts, updated 22 October 2026, tallies announcements made since October 2025 across tech, finance and consumer services. At the top sits HSBC, which said in March 2026 it would eliminate about 20,000 roles — roughly 10% of its workforce — as an AI overhaul begins. Amazon follows with 16,000 corporate job cuts announced in January 2026, described as an AI- and efficiency-driven restructuring. Barclays outlined about 5,000 reductions focused on support functions, aiming to save more than £1.1 billion. American Express announced about 3,500 global job cuts on 24 October 2026, a 5% workforce reduction tied to restructuring and AI investment, with a $336 million pre-tax charge. Some reports compute about 3,840 from 5% of AmEx's roughly 76,800 staff; this article uses the Reuters-carried count of about 3,500 and notes the gap rather than averaging two figures.

